    {
      "id": "ev_passage_9b698315ff523c84",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://lakefs.io/blog/best-vector-databases",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best 17 Vector Databases for 2026 [Top Picks]",
      "snippet": "### 12. Pgvector\n\n![Pgvector](https://i0.wp.com/lakefs.io/wp-content/uploads/2023/07/12-vector-databases-2023-pgvector.png?resize=1024%2C538&quality=80&ssl=1)\n\nPgvector: <https://github.com/pgvector/pgvector>\n\nOpen source? Yes\n\nGitHub stars: 4.5k\n\n**What problem does it solve?**\n\npgvector is a PostgreSQL extension for searching for vector similarity. You can also use it to keep embeddings as well. Ultimately, pgvector helps you store all of the application data in one place.\n\nIts users get to benefit from ACID compliance, point-in-time recovery, JOINs, and all of the other fantastic features we love PostgreSQL for.\n\n**Key features**\n\n* Exact and approximate nearest neighbor search\n* L2 distance, inner product, and cosine distance\n* any language with a PostgreSQL client",
      "content_ref": "21bc84abc0e94b56058a477b295ce50d385d76aca852d2236bdb109b110afe7b",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:43.712110+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 20815,
        "end_char": 21594,
