    {
      "id": "ev_passage_0fdd3b36b0a26db2",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-databases-compared",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "How to Choose the Right Vector Database: A Comparison Guide",
      "snippet": "[Vector databases](https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-database/) have become an invaluable part of many modern AI systems, powering everything from [AI chatbots](https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/a-technological-guide-to-building-an-ai-chatbot/) and [recommendation engines](https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/recommender-system-personalization/) to [fraud detection](https://www.altexsoft.com/whitepapers/fraud-detection-how-machine-learning-systems-help-reveal-scams-in-fintech-healthcare-and-ecommerce/) systems and intelligent document search.\n\nIn this article, we will explore some of the most popular and widely adopted solutions in the market, namely Chroma, Pinecone, Qdrant, Milvus, Weaviate, pgvector, MongoDB, and FAISS, to see how they compare so you can find the right fit for your stack.",
      "content_ref": "165e9229896e9480e913765353c95701f415547c0a07b526d3d9feb0a21e8d6f",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.488637+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 711,
        "end_char": 1508
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_63193a26005c8619",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-databases-compared",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "How to Choose the Right Vector Database: A Comparison Guide",
      "snippet": "The table below summarizes the key insights we’ll cover in detail.\n\n![vector databases](https://www.altexsoft.com/static/content-image/2026/4/vector-databases-123d3.png)\n\nVector databases compared\n\nA quick primer on vector databases\n----------------------------------\n\nVector databases store, manage, and query mathematical representations of [unstructured data](https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/unstructured-data/)—such as text, images, and audio. These representations, known as vector [embeddings](https://www.altexsoft.com/glossary/embedding/), are sequences of numbers that capture the meaning and characteristics of a data point in relation to others. As a result, similar items end up close together in a multidimensional space, while dissimilar ones are placed further apart.",
      "content_ref": "6e30c9c4a71fff0d43884a9c39a3db9d20e9e87e06a89fa9655a23cbababec8b",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.488637+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 1510,
        "end_char": 2291
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_b5e3faea6f5e2ad4",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-databases-compared",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "How to Choose the Right Vector Database: A Comparison Guide",
      "snippet": "**Resource-based pricing**, used by platforms like Qdrant Cloud and Weaviate Cloud, ties cost to allocated resources such as CPU, memory, and disk (i.e., cluster size). This is often more predictable for steady workloads but can be less efficient when resources are underutilized or traffic is highly variable.\n\n**Self-hosted setups** (e.g., with Milvus or pgvector) don’t have licensing fees, but incur infrastructure and engineering costs that can become significant as systems grow.\n\nWhen estimating cost, don’t focus only on your current dataset. Model how expenses change as your data volume, query load, and replication needs increase. A solution that is inexpensive at the prototype stage may not remain cost-effective in production.",
      "content_ref": "316e4a60bec1183a69648ac9edcc46e46e1765047249867bd5239ff9d0b6922f",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.488637+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 10251,
        "end_char": 10991,
        "heading": "Price structure and total cost of ownership",
        "heading_path": [
          "Price structure and total cost of ownership"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_e7ff2bac454f3a58",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-databases-compared",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "How to Choose the Right Vector Database: A Comparison Guide",
      "snippet": "### Deployment options\n\nUnlike many vector databases that offer only self-hosted or managed setups, Qdrant provides a broader range of deployment models, giving teams more flexibility in balancing control, compliance, and operational overhead.\n\n**Local / self-hosted.** Runs Qdrant via Docker or a binary for development, testing, or full production on your own infrastructure.\n\n**Qdrant Edge.** It’s a [lightweight mode](https://qdrant.tech/edge/) designed to run directly on devices (e.g., IoT gateways or offline systems) without requiring a full server setup.\n\n**Qdrant Cloud.** A fully managed service that handles scaling, updates, monitoring, backups, and availability across major cloud providers (AWS, GCP, Azure).",
      "content_ref": "1a024745727c6b94d7a67957391f70b61aadc53fadade2fc25ef70e28aa65cbc",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.488637+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 24684,
        "end_char": 25407,
        "heading": "Deployment options",
        "heading_path": [
          "Deployment options"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_8ca55ad50b83d659",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://www.altexsoft.com/blog/vector-databases-compared",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "How to Choose the Right Vector Database: A Comparison Guide",
      "snippet": "[Milvus](https://milvus.io/) is a cloud-native vector database written in Go and C++. It is one of the most popular open-source vector databases, with 42k+ GitHub stars. It supports a wide range of index types for dense, binary, and sparse vectors, including FLAT, IVF, HNSW, and DiskANN.\n\n![Attu's Vector Search interface, showing a hybrid search with results displaying similarity scores and scalar metadata](https://www.altexsoft.com/static/content-image/2026/3/attu-s-vector-search-interface-showing-a-hybrid-se-23cde.png)\n\nAttu's Vector Search interface, showing a hybrid search with results displaying similarity scores and scalar metadata. Source: [Attu](https://github.com/zilliztech/attu)",
      "content_ref": "9b2a2ba2666b2dcd9a9117e57f789929ce83479322fb27306d402b06c8386c5b",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.488637+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 29830,
        "end_char": 30527,
        "heading": "What actually differentiates Qdrant",
        "heading_path": [
          "What actually differentiates Qdrant"
        ]
      }
