    {
      "id": "ev_passage_e1165e39df7bb1ac",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "1. **Query Encoding**: Your search query (text, image, whatever) gets converted into a vector using the same embedding model that created your stored vectors.\n2. **Index Navigation**: The search algorithm uses your index structure to quickly narrow down candidates. This is the magic step—where your index determines whether you're checking 1,000 candidates or 1,000,000.\n3. **Distance Calculation**: For the candidate vectors, calculate actual distance (cosine similarity, euclidean distance, or dot product, depending on your use case).\n4. **Result Ranking**: Return the top K closest vectors, which correspond to your most similar documents, images, or whatever you're searching.",
      "content_ref": "2dcd0a76a45742df62c7109e5ccfc32ace30e8e5fba61c813b8844ae8ec38398",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 5599,
        "end_char": 6281
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_79031e8751579b99",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "The critical insight: step 2 is where performance lives or dies. A naive linear scan makes step 2 trivial (no index needed) but makes step 3 computationally prohibitive. A good index makes step 2 slightly more complex but reduces the candidates in step 3 by orders of magnitude.\n\nWhen Scale Turns Your Solution Into a Problem\n---------------------------------------------\n\nHere's where that thread I read becomes relevant. The developer's story followed a predictable arc:\n\n**Month 1**: 10,000 vectors, pgvector works great. Queries are fast. Index builds in seconds. Team is happy.\n\n**Month 6**: 500,000 vectors. Queries are slower but acceptable. Index builds take a few minutes. Memory usage is climbing but manageable.",
      "content_ref": "b08788a396442923cfa3940a40140dc5bedac8cc842bc64cc568140503488556",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 6283,
        "end_char": 7005
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_72724e5401f30c07",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "The elephant in the room: you must specify the number of clusters upfront. Too few clusters, and each cluster becomes large, defeating the purpose of clustering. Too many clusters, and you need to search more clusters to maintain recall, again defeating the purpose.\n\nThat number—the cluster count—significantly impacts everything. It affects query speed (fewer clusters to search = faster, but less accurate). It affects recall (how many relevant results you actually find versus miss). It affects memory usage. And here's the kicker: there's no magic formula for the right number. It depends on your data distribution, your query patterns, your accuracy requirements.",
      "content_ref": "640a2f73748241205b954444fbce03b41a1c3afd217110e2538e18e6fcab05db",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 9623,
        "end_char": 10292,
        "heading": "IVFFlat: The Cluster-Based Approach",
        "heading_path": [
          "IVFFlat: The Cluster-Based Approach"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_26553b7aa6e732ea",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "#### The Bad\n\nMemory requirements during index builds are significantly higher. You're not just storing vectors and cluster assignments; you're building a complex graph with multiple layers of connections. Each vector maintains links to multiple neighbors at multiple layers. This graph structure resides in memory during construction.\n\nIndex creation is slow. Painfully slow. Building an HNSW index for millions of vectors can take hours or even days. Each new vector must find its place in the existing graph structure, which means traversing the graph, identifying neighbors, and updating connections. As the graph grows, each insertion becomes more expensive.",
      "content_ref": "8f5b6bc56784c4a038541df346846e140d504b859fe8399d8a32f5960e384cdf",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 12177,
        "end_char": 12840,
        "heading": "The Bad",
        "heading_path": [
          "HNSW: The Graph-Based Alternative",
          "The Bad"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_fcf6821c8104d1d8",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "The memory requirements aren't theoretical; they're real, and they'll take down your database if you're not careful. That developer in the thread I read? They discovered this the hard way when their database instance ran out of memory mid-index-build and crashed. Their application stopped serving queries. Their alarms went off. Their CTO asked uncomfortable questions.\n\nWhy Specialized Vector Databases Exist\n--------------------------------------\n\nThis is the moment in the story where you realize that maybe, just maybe, using a general-purpose relational database for vector search at scale isn't the optimal solution. It's like trying to deliver packages using a sports car—it works for a few deliveries, but eventually you need a truck.",
      "content_ref": "f1f212036efb560ecc04a38ee34cf853161601c829ac384edcb801773a56a5c1",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 12842,
        "end_char": 13585,
        "heading": "The Bad",
        "heading_path": [
          "HNSW: The Graph-Based Alternative",
          "The Bad"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_2fd43512c155310c",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "#### Why Choose Typesense\n\nYou're building an application that needs both traditional text search and vector capabilities. Maybe you're building a product search where you want typo tolerance, faceting, and semantic similarity. Typesense excels at this hybrid use case.\n\nYou value open-source flexibility. You can self-host Typesense on any infrastructure, modify it if needed, and avoid vendor lock-in. You're not dependent on a proprietary service that might change pricing or terms.\n\nYou need a simple developer experience. Typesense's API is intuitive. Its documentation is clear. Its defaults are sensible. You're not spending weeks tuning parameters or deciphering error messages.",
      "content_ref": "56c7333ae6d338fc268ab640c1569b82809add969f3c4b16e89524a28e8fb6d4",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 16701,
        "end_char": 17387,
        "heading": "Why Choose Typesense",
        "heading_path": [
          "Typesense: The Developer Experience Champion",
          "Why Choose Typesense"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_088848f74d8ff62c",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "#### Why Choose Pinecone\n\nYou're building AI-first applications requiring pure vector search at scale. Your core functionality is semantic search, recommendation engines, or similarity matching. You don't need traditional text search features—you need vectors done right.\n\nYou need predictable low latency. Pinecone optimizes for consistent performance. You're not seeing the variable query times that plague less specialized solutions. Your P99 latency matters as much as your P50.\n\nYou want zero infrastructure management. No clusters to configure, no shards to rebalance, no indexes to rebuild manually. Pinecone handles the operations, letting you focus on building features.",
      "content_ref": "4628761b56814da1401f6f983a25c9bb2e0a34d950e99d4d2712e3069212a96d",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 18720,
        "end_char": 19399,
        "heading": "Why Choose Pinecone",
        "heading_path": [
          "Pinecone: The Vector-First Philosophy",
          "Why Choose Pinecone"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_bd3b769d1bcd7230",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://amitavroy.com/articles/beyond-pgvector-choosing-the-right-vector-database-for-productions",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Beyond pgvector: Choosing the Right Vector Database for Production",
      "snippet": "**Start with the query pattern**: What are you searching? Pure semantic similarity? Hybrid text + vector? Metadata filtering combined with vectors? Your query pattern eliminates entire categories of solutions.\n\n**Consider your operational reality**: Do you have a team that can run and tune complex distributed systems? Or are you three developers shipping fast? Your operational capacity determines how much complexity you can handle.\n\n**Think about scale—honestly**: That's where the thread I read got it right. The developer knew they were growing fast but hoped pgvector would scale. Hope is not a strategy. If you're expecting rapid growth, choose the solution that handles that growth gracefully, even if it's more complex initially.",
      "content_ref": "e2aed903eb96e7aa82a244f9029cbbddec45bfb7495da42f1560693da0f16bb7",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.599268+00:00",
