    {
      "id": "ev_passage_17fb237fb2716c3a",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best Open Source Vector Databases 2026 & Comparison",
      "snippet": "### **Milvus**\n\nMilvus is a cloud-native distributed vector database built for horizontal scaling across multiple nodes. Version 2.0 introduced a microservices architecture targeting large enterprises with massive vector workloads. It's Apache 2.0 licensed.\n\nThe tradeoff is operational complexity. Milvus Distributed is commonly deployed on Kubernetes for production at scale, though Milvus Standalone on a single machine is available for workloads that don't require distributed infrastructure. Organizations already running Kubernetes may find the distributed mode straightforward; teams without that expertise face a steeper learning curve.",
      "content_ref": "5d62e28598e53053fae5e76414a626a275928042dab238f2dfbfbc9fa9009334",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 9298,
        "end_char": 9942,
        "heading": "**Milvus**",
        "heading_path": [
          "**Milvus**"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_5dc7c01970c73421",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best Open Source Vector Databases 2026 & Comparison",
      "snippet": "### **pgvector (PostgreSQL Extension)**\n\npgvector adds vector search capabilities to existing PostgreSQL deployments, keeping everything in one system for teams already running PostgreSQL. Performance has improved with recent versions: [pgvector 0.8.0](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/supercharging-vector-search-performance-and-relevance-with-pgvector-0-8-0-on-amazon-aurora-postgresql/) delivers up to 5.7x improvement in query performance for specific patterns. In AWS benchmarks on a 10 million product dataset, filtered query latency dropped from 120ms to 70ms. The tradeoff is that pgvector requires PostgreSQL tuning expertise for vector workloads and doesn't include caching capabilities.",
      "content_ref": "a449f355019046720aa3ecfa3026429a5e9490f0fea9bdaed930d37ecda33a01",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 12251,
        "end_char": 12953,
        "heading": "**pgvector (PostgreSQL Extension)**",
        "heading_path": [
          "**pgvector (PostgreSQL Extension)**"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_5e99e0eb96aef2fa",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best Open Source Vector Databases 2026 & Comparison",
      "snippet": "### **Faiss (Meta AI Research)**\n\n[Faiss](https://engineering.fb.com/2017/03/29/data-infrastructure/faiss-a-library-for-efficient-similarity-search/) is a C++ library for efficient similarity search and clustering of dense vectors. It's explicitly designed as a library, not a database. That means you get algorithms, not infrastructure.\n\nTeams adopting Faiss must build persistence, backup and recovery, replication, and monitoring on their own. These are infrastructure components that purpose-built vector databases provide natively. For research environments or teams with strong C++ engineering resources who want maximum control over implementation details, that tradeoff can make sense.",
      "content_ref": "9898d0edb4e695c66becf7c46936dafa7aea15380793a78904c953362489f3fa",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 13232,
        "end_char": 13925,
        "heading": "**Faiss (Meta AI Research)**",
        "heading_path": [
          "**Faiss (Meta AI Research)**"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_fea4dcd6fe4bad9d",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best Open Source Vector Databases 2026 & Comparison",
      "snippet": "Each vector database in this comparison solves a specific problem. Milvus handles massive distributed workloads if you have Kubernetes expertise (or use standalone mode for simpler deployments). Qdrant delivers strong filtering with competitive latency. pgvector keeps everything in PostgreSQL and now handles tens of millions of vectors effectively. Chroma gets you prototyping fast.\n\nThe bigger question is whether you want a specialized vector store or a unified platform. Specialized tools do one thing well but add operational complexity; you'll need separate systems for caching, session management, and operational data. A unified approach puts vectors alongside everything else your AI app needs.",
      "content_ref": "5f9ac496918ede1b26c846ec90b28fd9715c82ef7d124463a9f03aca6bcc4a36",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 16418,
        "end_char": 17122,
        "heading": "**What deployment expertise does your team have?**",
        "heading_path": [
          "**What deployment expertise does your team have?**"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_85b3a9d7851c4440",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Best Open Source Vector Databases 2026 & Comparison",
      "snippet": "Redis takes the unified path: sub-100ms vector search in production deployments plus caching, operational data structures, and semantic caching in one system. That architectural consolidation often matters more than marginal differences in pure vector search benchmarks. You get low latency without Kubernetes complexity, semantic caching that [cuts LLM costs](https://redis.io/langcache/) by up to 70% in high-traffic applications, and one platform for vectors, caching, and operational data.\n\n[Try Redis free](https://redis.io/try-free/) to test vector search performance with your actual embeddings, or [talk to our team](https://redis.io/meeting/) about your AI infrastructure needs.\n\nSections\n\n[What makes a vector database production-ready](#What_makes_a_vector_database_productionready)",
      "content_ref": "415c26ba0b6438ce3ab247e57d41e031806325ee32363c2c2740fce14ed70ec5",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
