    {
      "id": "ev_passage_8657493ad70d4ee6",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://liveblocks.io/blog/whats-the-best-vector-database-for-building-ai-products",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "What's the best vector database for building AI products? | Liveblocks blog",
      "snippet": "It's open source and free to use. Costs come down to whatever infrastructure\nyou're running Postgres on. For teams already running Postgres in production,\nthis is a low-friction entry point, although studying the different indexing\noptions will be prudent. Managing a vector database on pgvector is not as easy\nas a dedicated option that's targeted specifically for vector search. It also\ncomes preinstalled on many popular vendors such as\n[Supabase](https://supabase.com/docs/guides/database/extensions/pgvector),\n[AWS](https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/database/leverage-pgvector-and-amazon-aurora-postgresql-for-natural-language-processing-chatbots-and-sentiment-analysis/),\nand [Neon](https://neon.com/docs/extensions/pgvector).",
      "content_ref": "2720fe04190ba081c7094a6b01b661aa2079d29c0761f5557f2c4e8fb00a79fb",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.326656+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 17775,
        "end_char": 18502,
        "heading": "[Features](#features)",
        "heading_path": [
          "[Features](#features)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_01f7855eadfd1414",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://liveblocks.io/blog/whats-the-best-vector-database-for-building-ai-products",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "What's the best vector database for building AI products? | Liveblocks blog",
      "snippet": "### [Features](#features)\n\nMilvus supports distributed deployments on Kubernetes and includes more indexing\nstrategies than any other competitor we could find such as IVF, HNSW, and\nDiskANN. It's best suited for enterprise-scale use cases where infrastructure is\nnot a bottleneck. Costs come down to your infrastructure and operations\ncomplexity. Milvus uses collections rather than namespaces.\n\nZilliz has a [pricing calculator](https://zilliz.com/pricing#calculator).\nPricing for serverless 1536 dimension vector with 1 million reads and 1 million\nwrites is $89. There is also a dedicated version which estimates a cost of $114.\nThey also offer a free plan with up to 5gb of storage.\n\nZilliz has SOC2 compliance and available SLAs.",
      "content_ref": "26300c0ca75325dc62633695ec6815c4066bf0f613592008b7dfaa8d41e80f23",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.326656+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 22932,
        "end_char": 23665,
        "heading": "[Features](#features)",
        "heading_path": [
          "[Features](#features)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_a8e348e0ddacb9f9",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://liveblocks.io/blog/whats-the-best-vector-database-for-building-ai-products",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "What's the best vector database for building AI products? | Liveblocks blog",
      "snippet": "### [Features](#features)\n\nMuch like pgvector and sqlite-vec, MongoDB's solution exists within a database\nyou may already be familiar with. As with those solutions, be prepared for\nincreased memory usage for vector indexes.\n\nPricing is difficult to calculate with atlas as their calculator does not have\nvector specific pricing and it's usage based on instance size. MongoDB does have\na free community edition under the\n[Server Side Public License](https://www.mongodb.com/legal/licensing/server-side-public-license).\n\nMongoDB has many options for clients, supports\n[hybrid searching](https://www.mongodb.com/docs/atlas/atlas-vector-search/hybrid-search/),\nand is only limited by scaling strategy and hardware.\n\n---\n\n[Chroma](#chroma)\n-----------------\n\n[trychroma.com](https://www.trychroma.com)",
      "content_ref": "e5c5b418db71e8f1254501b28f9fd0b60325804d3338fc8654aa08522981ec3a",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.326656+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 26234,
        "end_char": 27030,
        "heading": "[Features](#features)",
        "heading_path": [
          "[Features](#features)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_30bc48ee11035849",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://liveblocks.io/blog/whats-the-best-vector-database-for-building-ai-products",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "What's the best vector database for building AI products? | Liveblocks blog",
      "snippet": "### [Features](#features)\n\nChroma has full-text, metadata, and vector search available. Rather than\nnamespaces, it uses collections and above that is databases and tenants.\nInterestingly, internally Chroma uses\n[sqlite](https://cookbook.chromadb.dev/core/concepts/#distance-function) and\nobject storage much of its functionality.\n\nChroma has many SDK clients as well as an HTTP API available for use with any\nlanguage. Chroma's documentation is lacking in some areas, especially the\nopen-source clients, but it's simple enough that we found getting started was no\nproblem.\n\nChroma's cloud offering has simple usage based pricing with a nice pricing\n[calculator](https://www.trychroma.com/pricing) which works out to $81 for 1536\ndimension vector with 1 million writes and 1 million queries.\n\n---",
      "content_ref": "9abed5b339f8abb0d97f18c09b56ee8db6e1a24d6a8e6f0f2e56f14f37979456",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.326656+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 27161,
        "end_char": 27956,
        "heading": "[Features](#features)",
        "heading_path": [
          "[Features](#features)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_d36f47777624f24b",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "st but costly and limited. * Disk-based = larger scale, slower but persistent. * Hybrid (memory + disk) balances both. * Store only embeddings in vector DB; offload large documents elsewhere. |\n| **Deployment Model** | * Fully managed service vs. self-hosted * In-process vs. standalone server | * Managed: minimal ops, faster deployment, higher cost & less control. * Self-hosted: full control, cheaper at scale, higher ops burden. * Start embedded → move to networked as system scales. |\n| **Tenant Model** | * Single-tenant vs. multi-tenant architecture | * Single-tenant: simpler, faster. * Multi-tenant: cost-efficient but adds isolation and scaling complexity. * Use namespaces/collections for isolation if needed. |\n| **Query Features** | * Metadata filters * Hybrid (dense + sparse) search *",
      "content_ref": "ff24df134893c142bdf19c099d7dd7a12e9a871d95462a92a7c5c764aeab11ae",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 2339,
        "end_char": 3139
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_b4bcad4e5c2d9064",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "Multi-vector or mixture-of-encoders * Specialized queries (geo, facets) | * Strong filtering support is critical for scalability. * Hybrid search merges semantics + keywords. * Multi-vector support or mixture-of-encoders simplifies multi-modal search. * Few DBs support geo/faceted search natively. |\n| **Indexing Strategy** | * ANN vs. brute-force * HNSW, IVF, PQ, LSH variants * Index rebuild costs | * ANN offers massive speed-up with small recall trade-off. * Brute-force only for small datasets or accuracy-critical cases. * Evaluate on latency-recall curve, not index name. * Index rebuilds can be expensive; plan for them. |\n| **Operational Costs** | * Expensive ops: index rebuilds, bulk inserts, unindexed filters, strict consistency * Cheap ops: flat inserts, ANN queries, buffered writes,",
      "content_ref": "0299ea37d6e78f2e8bf9480ca0948e2e9b73cbe57f2a680377cb92758f5da0be",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 3139,
        "end_char": 3939
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_834beb8a425a8d71",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "* **Deployment Model:** Production deployments often demand a standalone or distributed database service rather than an embedded library. Fully managed services handle infrastructure scaling, replication, and maintenance for you, which accelerates deployment and reduces DevOps burden. You focus on using the database, not running it. In contrast, self-hosting gives complete control (important for data privacy or custom tuning) but means you manage servers, updates, and scaling yourself. The trade-off comes down to ease against control.\n* **Ephemeral vs. Durable:** During prototyping you might tolerate an ephemeral in-memory index. This can be faster since it avoids disk I/O, but it’s not suitable for production where persistence and recovery are required. For large-scale applications, ensur",
      "content_ref": "55d9c1a37f0c94b6cc35e299a60f1e94574de8db69a9deaf41ff0b8a35da4947",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 4858,
        "end_char": 5658
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_f3f9f198daf00f9b",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "The same prototype to production migration applies to inference. Local encode calls are fine at the start, but they become hard to manage when you need multiple models, dedicated rerankers and predictable p99 latency. An inference layer such as SIE centralises model lifecycle management, batching, memory-safe multi-model serving and hot reload. That lets you run multi-output encoders and dedicated rerankers in production without rewriting your retrieval path.\n\nUse lightweight embedded solutions for early development. As you scale, plan for a production deployment: either a self-managed cluster tuned for your needs or a managed cloud service that offloads maintenance.\n\n---\n\nWrite-Heavy vs. Read-Heavy Workloads\n------------------------------------",
      "content_ref": "648923603781ddb3317ed26e5b9b42570c0d0860f373fecdc3a6941493540403",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 5749,
        "end_char": 6504
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_2fee9287f80d0005",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "* **High Write Throughput:** If you need to index new vectors continuously, look for systems optimized for fast inserts and updates. Some indexes can handle incremental additions but with caveats (e.g. HNSW insert performance degradation over time). Many databases separate write/read paths by buffering new vectors and merging later.\n* **Read-Heavy Patterns:** If your dataset is mostly static, you can use heavier, more advanced index structures to accelerate reads. This adds memory overhead but delivers very low-latency queries at scale.\n* **Balancing Both:** Many real-time applications use a hybrid: a mutable index for recent data and a static ANN index for older data. Queries hit both, ensuring freshness + performance.",
      "content_ref": "6c86bf701d97515dcc735e007986a9e09dca5ab471b387bb85ff9d5fe6970d0c",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 6739,
        "end_char": 7468
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_d5dd33057d0a8c0c",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "* **Fully Managed Services:** Cloud-hosted vector DB offerings where the provider runs the infrastructure. They handle scaling, replication, upgrades, and often provide high-level APIs. Ideal when you want to integrate semantic search quickly without building ML ops expertise. The trade-off is less customization and potentially higher long-term cost.\n* **Self-Hosted (Open Source or Enterprise):** Running your own vector database (or using an open-source library) gives maximum flexibility and control, at the cost of operational complexity and DevOps investment.\n* **In-Process Libraries vs. Standalone Servers:** Embedded libraries are great for prototyping or single-application usage with no network overhead. Standalone services are better for multi-client architectures or horizontal scaling",
      "content_ref": "b661302805eb28b79a99a790107ffaf6deca80b4ebb8a9e3ac2e8e70549fbb41",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 9799,
        "end_char": 10599
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_12ee9c6dc288456c",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "* **Single-Tenant (Simplicity):** One large corpus or a few related ones, in one or a few indexes. Straightforward to manage and typically higher per-query performance.\n* **Multi-Tenant Support:** A single cluster serving many isolated datasets. Enables cost sharing but adds complexity around isolation, quotas, indexing strategies, and scaling.\n\nDesign for multi-tenancy only if you need to. A vector DB that shines for one big dataset may not handle thousands of tiny ones efficiently, and vice versa.\n\n---\n\nSearch Features and Query Functionality\n---------------------------------------\n\nNot all vector databases offer the same query capabilities beyond basic nearest neighbor search. Consider what search functionality your application requires:",
      "content_ref": "d514e5c608e76ade07b14f6bd199b6b72b6f086dc093a79c5be886f50d439e7e",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 11877,
        "end_char": 12627
      }
