    {
      "id": "ev_passage_1929a490ad05dec3",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "# present top results\n\n\n\nranked = sorted(zip(hits, scores), key=lambda x: x[1], reverse=True)[:10]\n\n\n\nfor hit, score in ranked:\n\n\n\nprint(f\"{score:.3f}\\t{hit.payload.get('title', '')}\\t{hit.payload.get('text')[:180]}\")\n```\n\n---\n\nIndexing Strategies vs. Query Performance\n-----------------------------------------\n\n![](/cdn/65dce6831bf9f730421e2915/blog/choosing-vdb-accuracy-query.png)\n\nAt the heart of any vector database is the index type it uses for nearest neighbor search. You’ll encounter terms like brute-force (flat) search, HNSW graphs, IVF, PQ, LSH, and others. Rather than focusing on names, focus on how index choice affects query latency and recall:",
      "content_ref": "f48c7f3d3e8424bd9a413f147e979293debe4584360e084cf456f37f73ee90a2",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 14270,
        "end_char": 14931,
        "heading": "present top results",
        "heading_path": [
          "present top results"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_e03f746ece98679a",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://superlinked.com/blog/choosing-a-vector-database",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "A Practical Guide for Choosing a Vector Database | Superlinked Blog",
      "snippet": "* **Brute-Force vs. Approximate:** Brute-force delivers exact results but does not scale. ANN trades a bit of accuracy for orders-of-magnitude faster queries and is standard at scale.\n* **Index Types:** Different structures (HNSW, IVF, PQ, DiskANN, etc.) have different strengths but all live on a latency/recall trade-off curve. The important question is whether the DB can hit your targets on that curve.\n* **Guaranteed Recall:** For cases demanding 100 percent recall, a common pattern is ANN for candidates plus exact reranking on a smaller subset.\n\nUsing an inference score endpoint for reranking keeps heavy compute off the vector DB and ensures consistent reranking across datasets.",
      "content_ref": "6b316b2008278e74647696c352c7236718188bc98f0a0595de64ded335365bac",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:41.614611+00:00",
