      "url": "https://thedataquarry.com/blog/vector-db-4",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Vector databases (4): Analyzing the trade-offs • The Data Quarry",
      "snippet": "### 1. On-prem vs. cloud hosting[#](#1-on-prem-vs-cloud-hosting)\n\nA lot of vendors stress on their “cloud-native” feature as though it’s the best thing since sliced bread, from a scalability perspective. However, in deciding what solution works best from the *cost* perspective, it’s important to consider how the hosting options are broken down in the first place. Embedded databases architectures are part of the options now! 🎉\n\nConsider the following combinations:\n\n* Cloud-native (managed) + client-server\n* On-prem (self-hosted) + embedded\n* Cloud-native (managed) + embedded\n\nIt’s easier to compare these combinations visually.\n\n ![](/_astro/vector-db-hosting-2.8933CK0Z_13YxTB.webp)",
      "content_ref": "a15659a0cf7e7108f0ea1a8b09a98039b88a2283ae30e4fa4cd6b08ae4149c8a",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.427708+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 2591,
        "end_char": 3281,
        "heading": "1. On-prem vs. cloud hosting[#](#1-on-prem-vs-cloud-hosting)",
        "heading_path": [
          "1. On-prem vs. cloud hosting[#](#1-on-prem-vs-cloud-hosting)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_8fb5a07ad30e13e2",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://thedataquarry.com/blog/vector-db-4",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Vector databases (4): Analyzing the trade-offs • The Data Quarry",
      "snippet": "### 3. Insertion speed vs. query speed[#](#3-insertion-speed-vs-query-speed)\n\nCertain vendors like Milvus/Zilliz, have been around for long enough that they are looking at humongous-scale streaming use cases that require vectors. Inserting, and then indexing thousands of vectors per minute may be essential in real-time scenarios like video surveillance or financial transaction tracking, and Milvus/Zilliz are capable of throwing the kitchen sink at this problem[6](#user-content-fn-5).\n\nHowever, is insertion speed that important to the majority of use cases? For most organizations, querying speed is more important. This is because insertion and indexing are typically done infrequently, but the data might be queried much more frequently, typically in real-time at scale via user interfaces.",
      "content_ref": "ba8b158e9bd4f47ba7fe35e90b80461170ce7794ad6e6845068ff51e4efd8662",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.427708+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 9190,
        "end_char": 9987,
        "heading": "3. Insertion speed vs. query speed[#](#3-insertion-speed-vs-query-speed)",
        "heading_path": [
          "3. Insertion speed vs. query speed[#](#3-insertion-speed-vs-query-speed)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_0125f43418ea2889",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://thedataquarry.com/blog/vector-db-4",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Vector databases (4): Analyzing the trade-offs • The Data Quarry",
      "snippet": "Qdrant, an open-source, purpose-built vector DB written in Rust 🦀, optimizes exactly for this use case. Because it’s written in Rust, it performs admirably fast in indexing as well, but, as shown in the Qdrant demo on their own documentation page, semantic search-as-you-type, returning vector search solutions that produce relevant results in just a few milliseconds, is actually achievable in a live setting. 🤯\n\n ![](/_astro/qdrant-search-as-you-type.CA0JoC96_8Eltq.webp) \n\n[Qdrant Twitter](https://twitter.com/qdrant_engine/status/1691054852913418241)\n\n \n\nQuestions to ask:\n\n* How often am I going to be inserting (and indexing) a large number of vectors?\n* Do I meet the latency requirements of my application at query time?",
      "content_ref": "ea4be25c4fe20043010bcb9ffd24ea2ff60ea4d781052446ea28ee22592a20ac",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.427708+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 9989,
        "end_char": 10717,
        "heading": "3. Insertion speed vs. query speed[#](#3-insertion-speed-vs-query-speed)",
        "heading_path": [
          "3. Insertion speed vs. query speed[#](#3-insertion-speed-vs-query-speed)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "ev_passage_61eac37ab5f86e57",
      "source_type": "passage",
      "provider": "direct_http",
      "url": "https://thedataquarry.com/blog/vector-db-4",
      "document_id": null,
      "title": "Vector databases (4): Analyzing the trade-offs • The Data Quarry",
      "snippet": "### 5. In-memory vs. on-disk index and vector storage[#](#5-in-memory-vs-on-disk-index-and-vector-storage)\n\nDatabases like Redis are completely in-memory, and are blazing fast. However, it’s very plausible that your use case requires storing enough vectors that are larger than memory. A combination of tricks is required to scale vector storage to very large data (hundreds of millions, or even billions of vectors) while also maintaining ANN search speed. Databases like Qdrant and Weaviate provide the option of using memory-mapped files for vectors that utilize the page cache’s virtual address space on disk, avoiding loading the entire data into RAM. This helps maintain almost the speed of in-memory databases without actually persisting the data to the disk.",
      "content_ref": "f833190f69965bcf34de9296ac0e5d437c4a3e24002b35b2962d27685c513e49",
      "published_date": null,
      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:42.427708+00:00",
      "query": null,
      "quality_score": null,
      "freshness_score": null,
      "citation_id": null,
      "metadata": {
        "start_char": 13013,
        "end_char": 13779,
        "heading": "5. In-memory vs. on-disk index and vector storage[#](#5-in-memory-vs-on-disk-index-and-vector-storage)",
        "heading_path": [
          "5. In-memory vs. on-disk index and vector storage[#](#5-in-memory-vs-on-disk-index-and-vector-storage)"
        ]
      }
    },
    {
