      "retrieved_at": "2026-05-04T17:25:40.790686+00:00",
      "method": "direct_http",
      "quote": "Each vector database in this comparison solves a specific problem. Milvus handles massive distributed workloads if you have Kubernetes expertise (or use standalone mode for simpler deployments). Qdrant delivers strong filtering with competi",
      "snippet_hash": "5f9ac496918ede1b26c846ec90b28fd9715c82ef7d124463a9f03aca6bcc4a36"
    },
    {
      "url": "https://redis.io/blog/best-open-source-vector-databases-comparison",
      "text": "Redis takes the unified path: sub-100ms vector search in production deployments plus caching, operational data structures, and semantic caching in one system. That architectural consolidation often matters more than marginal differences in pure vector search benchmarks. You get low latency without Kubernetes complexity, semantic caching that [cuts LLM costs](https://redis.io/langcache/) by up to 70% in high-traffic applications, and one platform for vectors, caching, and operational data.\n\n[Try Redis free](https://redis.io/try-free/) to test vector search performance with your actual embeddings, or [talk to our team](https://redis.io/meeting/) about your AI infrastructure needs.\n\nSections\n\n[What makes a vector database production-ready](#What_makes_a_vector_database_productionready)",
